<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lalakaon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/863">863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Poson · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-byzantine-wars">Arab–Byzantine wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lalakaon was fought on 3 September 863 in Paphlagonia, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, between a Byzantine army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronas">Petronas</a> and the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-al-aqta">Umar al-Aqta</a>, emir of Melitene, who had sacked Amisos on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> encircled the Arab army and destroyed it, killing Umar. The victory marked the start of Byzantium's offensive on its eastern frontier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lalakaon.jpg" alt="Battle of Lalakaon" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, 13th-century author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fighting_between_Byzantines_and_Arabs_Chronikon_of_Ioannis_Skylitzes,_end_of_13th_century..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Arab raids into Byzantine Asia Minor had come almost every year through the 8th and 9th centuries. After 842 the threat from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> waned, but semi-independent frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> remained dangerous, above all Melitene, whose position west of the Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a> gave direct access to the Anatolian plateau. In 860 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-al-aqta">Umar al-Aqta</a> and the Paulician leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karbeas">Karbeas</a> raided deep into Asia Minor. In the summer of 863 Umar joined the Abbasid general Ja'far ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinar">Dinar</a> al-Khayyat in a raid into Cappadocia and then pressed on alone. Ya'qubi gave him 8,000 men; the Byzantinist John Haldon puts the combined Arab forces at 15,000 to 20,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Emperor Michael III met the raiders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>'s Meadow, north of Nazianzus, in a bloody battle, but they escaped north into the Armeniac Theme and sacked Amisos. Michael then gathered a large force, al-Tabari claims 50,000 men, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronas">Petronas</a>, Domestic of the Schools, and Nasar, governor of the Bucellarian Theme. Three armies, from the northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a>, from the south and from the west under Petronas, converged on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and met on 2 September. Their position was most likely near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a>, about 130 kilometres south-east of Amisos.</p><p>During the night the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> won the hill that commanded the only way out. The next day Umar threw his whole force against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronas">Petronas</a>, whose men held firm while the other two armies struck the Arab rear and flanks. Most of the Arab army fell. Umar's son escaped with a small force and was captured by Machairas, commander of Charsianon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>That autumn another Byzantine army defeated and killed Ali al-Armani in Arab-held Armenia, and the power of Melitene was destroyed for good. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> hailed the victory as revenge for the sack of Amorium in 838; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronas">Petronas</a> was made magistros and Charsianon became a full theme. Al-Tabari reports grief and riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Freed from the eastern threat, the empire invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 864, and its ruler Boris accepted Byzantine missionaries and was baptised with the name Michael. Henri Grégoire held that these victories inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Armouris.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Lalakaon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its most famous ruler, Umar al-Aqta, raided deep into Asia Minor, allied with the Paulicians of Tephrike and in 863 sacked Amisos on the Black Sea before being killed at the <b>battle of Lalakaon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-melitene">Emirate of Melitene</a></li></ul></section>
